<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/ecology-populations-communities">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Ecology is biology with the setting included: how living things interact with each other and
    with their surroundings. Its basic unit is the <strong>population</strong>, whose growth obeys
    a simple tension — life multiplies exponentially, but resources don't. The <strong>carrying
    capacity</strong> of the environment bends every real growth curve toward an S-shape.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="exponential promise, logistic reality — the S-curve is ecology's first law" widget="plotter" config={ecoCfg} />

  <h2>From populations to communities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Niches:</strong> a species' role and address. Competitive exclusion (Gause, 1934) says two species cannot share one niche forever — which is why coexisting species always differ somehow (Darwin's finches are the textbook case).</li>
    <li><strong>Predator–prey cycles:</strong> hare and lynx populations (90 years of Hudson Bay fur records) oscillate in a lagged chase — the classic live demonstration of coupled dynamics.</li>
    <li><strong>Keystone species:</strong> sea otters keep urchins from mowing kelp forests; wolves restructured Yellowstone's rivers (trophic cascade). Communities are networks, and some nodes matter disproportionately.</li>
    <li><strong>Succession:</strong> bare rock → lichens → grasses → shrubs → forest — ecosystems assemble in a predictable order after disturbances, over decades to centuries.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Gause (1934); Odum, Fundamentals of Ecology; Krebs, Ecology; Yellowstone wolf studies (Ripple & Beschta).</p>
</TopicPage>
